% ============================================================================
% precipitation-alternate-form — for hail, the SECOND descriptive term an
% already-cited NWS Glossary sentence lists alongside its primary form,
% decoded as its own row (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `precipitation-types.adj`
% (`precip_form(precip, form)`, ONE defining physical form per precipitation
% type — hail → balls_of_ice) and `precipitation-minimum-diameter.adj`
% (`precipitation_min_diameter(precip, min_diameter_mm)`, decoding the same
% hail sentence's numeric threshold). That SAME already-quoted NWS Glossary
% sentence lists TWO descriptive terms for hail joined by "or" -- a fact the
% single `form` atom folded into one label, keeping only the second term:
%
%   hail (two alternatives joined by "or"):
%     "Showery precipitation in the form of irregular pellets or balls of
%      ice more than 5 mm in diameter, falling from a cumulonimbus cloud."
%
% `precip_form` already carries `balls_of_ice`; `irregular_pellets` never
% made it into any table. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? precipitation_alternate_form(hail, $Form)   % irregular_pellets
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `precipitation_alternate_form(precip, form)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query -- the engine returns the alternate form WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any precipitation type whose
% own cited span names only one descriptive term with no listed
% alternative. `rain`, `snow`, `sleet`, and `freezing_rain` are deliberately
% NOT rows here: none of their own already-quoted spans (in
% `precipitation-types.adj`'s header) lists a second alternative term for
% the SAME concept -- so this sibling is HONESTLY NARROWER than either
% parent, grounding exactly the one precipitation type whose cited span
% lists more than one descriptive term and abstaining on any other, rather
% than inventing one. The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a form and
% recall which precipitation type it names:
%
%     ? precipitation_alternate_form($Precip, irregular_pellets)
%       % hail
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NWS Glossary sentence
% `precipitation-types.adj`'s own header and `precipitation-minimum-diameter.adj`'s
% own header already quote in full for hail -- no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding the second listed term as its own row instead
% of folding the whole clause into one compound `form` atom. `trust
% authoritative` -- the same tier both parent tables already carry (NOAA
% NWS Glossary, a primary U.S. government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table precipitation_alternate_form {
    columns precip, form

    row (hail, irregular_pellets)

    source "Showery precipitation in the form of irregular pellets or balls of ice more than 5 mm in diameter, falling from a cumulonimbus cloud."
    locator "https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=HAIL"
    trust authoritative
}
